Z||000000||87GT||01-06-2001||09:20 PM||||I am thinking about getting some kind of suspension kit so I can improve the handling of my 87GT, I was just wondering if anybody here has bought a kit from the Fierostore and if so, tell me what you thing of it now. I would also like to hear from anyone who has gotten a kit from somewhere else.  Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||v8vinnie||01-06-2001||09:30 PM||||well so far i have ordered their bigger sway bar kits, big improvment.Then i orderd their kyb shocks and struts,( my rears were shot). NOW i ordered their rear poly bushings for the control arm and the cradle, putting them in tommorow, their prices are okay and their service and tech help is first rate!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||v8vinnie||01-06-2001||09:31 PM||||oh yea!, i also ordered their pro lowering spring kit.||||reg||1
Z||000003||v8vinnie||01-06-2001||09:33 PM||||and one more thing,THE TECH HELP FROME YOU GUYS OUY THERE IS ALSO FIRST RATE!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||01-07-2001||09:08 AM||||I ordered the entire kit at once. Lowering springs, KYB shocks/struts, and sway bars. I also ordered poly bushings and new ball joints, tie rods, bearings and rotors. The kit works great! Also if you mention that you are part of a Fiero Club you can get 10% off the kit so the price is not bad. If you are going to put a kit on, I would examine everything on the susupension. Replace anything worn at the same time so you don't have to pull it back apart. I just replaced everything from the start. I think the total cost was just over $1000.00 but it is all new and feels great! <BR>The Fiero Store kit is:<BR>Eibach springs<BR>KYB struts and shocks<BR>Suspension Tech. Sway bars<BR>The springs will lower car 1 to 1.5" <BR>the sway bars are bigger than stock.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Got2manyfieros||01-07-2001||02:48 PM||||I have the fiero store lowering springs (not the eibachs) and I origionally had KYB shocks, but they could not keep up with the springs, so I switched to Koni's and the car rides and handles much better.  Also the springs I have make the car VERY LOW so if you are from an area that requires much off roading, or negotiating speed bumps you may want to consider different springs.<P>I also have their cradle bushings, and front sway bar, no complaints on either.<P>The only suspention stuff on my car not from the fiero store are my adjustable front upper ball joints (-1 degree of camber) and my race tires (Kumho Victoracers).<P>How does it handle you ask?  Well, with a stock iron duke, I was turning 75 second laps at limerock park (1.55 mile road course).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||01-07-2001||07:54 PM||||Email correspondence from the Fiero Store...<P>Greg,<P>The Eibach's we sell are the Pro Kit.<P>Thanks<BR>The Fiero Store<P>----- Original Message ----- <BR>From: Greg Piet &lt;gregpiet@physics.unlv.edu&gt;<BR>To: &lt;info@fierostore.com&gt;<BR>Sent: Wednesday, January 03, 2001 11:20 PM<BR>Subject: Part inquiry<P><BR>&gt; I am considering purchasing your 84-87 Eibach lowering spring set PN<BR>&gt; 59014<BR>&gt; I was wondering if those springs are the Eibach "Pro Kit" or another<BR>&gt; spring kit, as Eibach advertises they have 3 different types of springs,<BR>&gt; namely the pro kit, the sportline kit and the roadrace kit.<BR>&gt; <BR>&gt; Thanks for your time,<BR>&gt; Greg Piet<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||v8vinnie||01-08-2001||08:31 PM||||hey greg, may be you can enlightened us on the 3 different spring kits like lowering ,spring rates, ect. i have the pro kit from you guys,whats the difference, motor heads like us want to know??????||||reg||1
